{"step_index":5671,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-25T10:55:01Z","thinking":"The process has reported an inode number (14555830). This value seems to exceed the expected maximum for the file system (14417920), which is causing an inconsistency. The analysis concludes that the command is likely near the end of the filesystem processing, and requires waiting for a related task to complete.\n\n\nAnalyzing file reconstruction, the identified directory tables, along with the inode numbers, will be instrumental in mapping the extracted files. The next step involves using the known file naming convention `I_00<inode_number>.<ext>` from the `MAGIC-2` directory to cross-reference and reconstruct files from their inode numbers.\n\n\nThe file system snapshot provides a directory listing with associated inode numbers across several project directories. This data will be critical in correlating files with their content when rebuilding the file structure. I will utilize the inode numbers to reconstruct the relationships between files and directories.\n\n\nThis listing of inodes and associated files will serve as a foundational inventory for reconstructing the file system. Each inode maps to a specific file, providing the necessary metadata for rebuilding the directory structure. Initial assessment suggests these files represent a diverse collection of data, system configurations, and application-specific content.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"python3 -c \\\"\\nimport struct\\nimport json\\n\\nwith open('/tmp/journal.copy', 'rb') as f:\\n    journal = f.read()\\n\\nBLOCK_SIZE = 4096\\nnum_blocks = len(journal) // BLOCK_SIZE\\n\\ndef parse_entries(block):\\n    pos = 0\\n    entries = []\\n    while pos < len(block):\\n        if pos + 8 > len(block):\\n            break\\n        ino, rlen, nlen, ft = struct.unpack('<IHBB', block[pos:pos+8])\\n        if rlen == 0 or pos + rlen > len(block):\\n            break\\n        if ino != 0 and 0 < nlen < 256:\\n            try:\\n                name = block[pos+8:pos+8+nlen].decode('utf-8')\\n                entries.append((ino, name, ft))\\n            except:\\n                pass\\n        pos += rlen\\n    return entries\\n\\n# Scan all blocks for directory entries to build global inode -> (parent_ino, name, ft) mapping\\ninode_map = {}\\nfor b_idx in range(num_blocks):\\n    block = journal[b_idx*BLOCK_SIZE : (b_idx+1)*BLOCK_SIZE]\\n    # Check if directory block\\n    if len(block) >= 24:\\n        ino1, rlen1, nlen1, ft1 = struct.unpack('<IHBB', block[0:8])\\n        if nlen1 == 1 and block[8:9] == b'.' and rlen1 == 12:\\n            entries = parse_entries(block)\\n            parent_ino = ino1\\n            for ino, name, ft in entries:\\n                if name not in ('.', '..') and ino not in inode_map:\\n                    inode_map[ino] = {'parent': parent_ino, 'name': name, 'type': ft}\\n\\nprint(f'Total mapped inodes from journal directory blocks: {len(inode_map)}')\\nwith open('/tmp/inode_map.json', 'w') as out:\\n    json.dump(inode_map, out, indent=2)\\n\\\"\"","Cwd":"\"/home/azureuser\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"8000","toolAction":"\"Building complete inode map from journal\"","toolSummary":"\"Build complete inode map from journal\""}}]}
